From: Balena RK3588 integration
Subject: [PATCH] rockchip_sdhci: report the eMMC CRU and controller state at probe

Diagnostic dump for the eMMC -95 investigation: print the raw CRU
clock-gate/select state for the eMMC clocks (CLKGATE_CON(31) bits 4-8
with CLK_GATE_SET_TO_DISABLE polarity - 0 means the clock is enabled;
CLKSEL_CON(77)/(78) are the cclk/bclk mux+div) together with the
controller capabilities, HOST_CONTROL/HOST_CONTROL2 and the vendor
EMMC_CONTROL register.  Also print the final CLOCK_CONTROL and
PRESENT_STATE after the identify clock is enabled, so a failing boot
reports the exact state at CMD time.

Note: an earlier revision of this patch wrote 0x1f0 to the gate bits
with inverted polarity and gated all five clocks off (caps read back
zero, "Your controller doesn't support SDMA!!"); the register dump
showed the gates were 0 (enabled) from reset, so nothing is written
any more.

+++ b/drivers/mmc/rockchip_sdhci.c
@@ -448,6 +448,11 @@
 	/* enable output clock */
 	sdhci_enable_clk(host, 0);
 
+	printf("%s: idclk final cc=%04x ps=%08x\n",
+	       priv->dev->name,
+	       sdhci_readw(host, SDHCI_CLOCK_CONTROL),
+	       sdhci_readl(host, SDHCI_PRESENT_STATE));
+
 	return ret;
 }
 
@@ -597,6 +602,25 @@
 	}
 
 	/*
+	 * Diagnostic only: dump the eMMC CRU clock state.  The five eMMC
+	 * gates live in CLKGATE_CON(31) bits 4-8 (hclk/aclk/cclk/bclk/
+	 * tmclk) with CLK_GATE_SET_TO_DISABLE polarity, so 0 there means
+	 * all five clocks are already enabled; nothing needs writing.
+	 */
+	{
+		/* RK3588 CRU; this vendor tree has no CRU syscon entry. */
+		void __iomem *cru = (void __iomem *)0xfd7c0000;
+
+		printf("%s: emmc clkgate31 %08x sel77 %08x sel78 %08x caps %08x hc %02x hc2 %04x ctl %08x\n",
+		       dev->name,
+		       readl(cru + 0x87c), readl(cru + 0x434), readl(cru + 0x438),
+		       sdhci_readl(host, SDHCI_CAPABILITIES),
+		       sdhci_readb(host, SDHCI_HOST_CONTROL),
+		       sdhci_readw(host, SDHCI_HOST_CONTROL2),
+		       sdhci_readl(host, DWCMSHC_EMMC_CONTROL));
+	}
+
+	/*
 	 * The kernel's rk35xx_sdhci_reset() pulses all device-tree reset
 	 * controls (core/bus/axi/block/timer) on SDHCI_RESET_ALL; a host
 	 * left wedged by earlier boot stages only recovers after that
